ASA-DSNet: A Dual-Stage Framework with
Ambiguous Sample Augmentation for Robust
Parkinson’s Disease Identification
Xinyue Xing, Ranxuan Tang, Yanmiao Kong, Aite Zhao* College of Computer Science and Technology, Qingdao University, Qingdao, China {xingxinyue, 2025021110, kongyanmiao, zhaoaite}@qdu.edu.cn
Abstract—Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a neurodegenerative disorder with rising prevalence, and early diagnosis is critical to slowing disease progression. Traditional diagnostic methods rely on clinical rating scales, which are inherently subjective and prone to misdiagnosis. This paper proposes ASA-DSNet, a dualstage learning framework for PD detection using smartwatch motion data. The first stage (M1) fuses multi-scale time-frequency features and identifies ambiguous samples (i.e., boundary and misclassified samples), which are augmented using the Ambiguous Sample Augmenter (ASA). The second stage (M2) employs hierarchical attention mechanisms to refine decision boundaries. Experimental results demonstrate that ASA-DSNet achieves accuracies of 80.85% and 95.17% on the PADS and Monipar datasets, outperforming representative baselines. This model provides a viable solution for early PD screening and tremor grading. Index Terms—Parkinson’s disease, smartwatch inertial data, dual-stage learning, ambiguous sample augmentation.
I. INTRODUCTION
As the global population ages, the prevalence of neurodegenerative disorders, particularly PD, continues to increase [1]. The hallmark motor symptoms of PD, including resting tremor, rigidity, and bradykinesia, significantly hinder patients’ ability to carry out daily activities [2], [3]. In the absence of a definitive cure for PD, early and reliable diagnosis is pivotal to delaying disease progression [4]. However, traditional diagnosis relies heavily on clinical scales (e.g., UPDRS) and physician experience, which poses significant limitations [5]. A recent comprehensive meta-analysis reported that up to 26% of patients were initially misdiagnosed according to the clinical diagnostic criteria for PD [6]. This high misdiagnosis rate stems from two main factors: the heterogeneity and overlap of clinical symptoms–PD often shares features with diseases like essential tremor and multiple system atrophy [3]; and the inherent bias of subjective assessment: human vision struggles to quantify subtle motor characteristics. Given the limitations of conventional diagnostic methods, there is an urgent need for an objective and accurate tool to detect motor features of PD. Wearable devices, especially smartwatches, have emerged as a promising solution due to
