their convenience, high-precision data acquisition, and unobtrusiveness in daily life [7], [8]. At present, many approaches for PD detection using smartwatch data integrate traditional techniques with deep learning algorithms [6], [9]. However, most of these methods overlook a class of ambiguous samples: those located near the decision boundary with highly overlapping features, or those misclassified by the current model. Additionally, existing PD diagnostics exhibit significant shortcomings at the data level, primarily relying on binary classification between healthy controls (HC) and PD patients [10], [11]. In real-world clinical tasks, however, it is necessary to distinguish PD from its differential diagnoses (DD) that share overlapping symptoms, such as essential tremor and multiple system atrophy, which makes accurate classification more challenging. To address the inherent algorithmic and data limitations in existing PD detection methods, we propose a dual-stage framework that refines model decision boundaries and optimizes training data. Specifically, we introduce a Dual-Stage Optimization Framework and an Ambiguous Sample Augmenter. These techniques work in tandem to enhance classification accuracy and improve model robustness when dealing with complex, overlapping categories.
II. METHODS
A. Framework Overview
As illustrated in Fig. 1, the proposed framework operates in two stages. Stage 1 (M1) serves as the primary classifier, extracting coupled temporal–frequency representations from raw upper-limb motion and producing initial predictions. An Ambiguous Sample Augmenter (ASA) subsequently identifies both misclassified and boundary cases, adaptively assigning higher training weights based on their proximity to the decision surface. Stage 2 (M2) acts as a corrective refiner with hierarchical attention, focusing capacity on these hard samples to sharpen decision boundaries and improve overall discrimination.
