D. Stage 2 (M2): Corrective Refiner
The Stage 2 model M2 is based on the MLSTM-FCN backbone and integrates Convolutional Block Attention Modules (CBAM) to refine misclassified and boundary samples mined by ASA. Unlike traditional classifiers, M2 focuses on hard decision regions and sharpens decision boundaries using lightweight attention. Given the feature map F ∈ RT ×C , channel attention calculates a channel mask M c via global pooling, enhancing informative channels and suppressing irrelevant ones:
M c = σ MLP AvgPool(F ) + MaxPool(F ) (12)
Temporal attention highlights salient time steps:
M t = σ Conv1D [AvgPool(F ); MaxPool(F )] (13)
At each stage, M c and M t are applied multiplicatively to recalibrate the feature map. Trained on the ASA-weighted set, M2 focuses on regions where M1 errs or shows low confidence, refining decision boundaries and improving overall robustness.
III. EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS
A. Datasets
This study is primarily based on the publicly available PADS dataset [12], which supports a multiclass classification task involving PD, DD and HC. To further evaluate the performance and generalizability of the proposed framework across different PD classification scenarios, we additionally conducted experiments on the Monipar dataset [13]. Unlike PADS, the Monipar dataset focuses on a different yet clinically relevant task-classifying tremor severity levels in PD patientsthus providing an opportunity to verify the adaptability and robustness of our method in diverse application settings.
B. Experimental Setup
To obtain unbiased estimates and avoid data leakage, we adopt a nested cross-validation protocol with Kouter=5 outer folds for evaluation and Kinner=5 inner folds for model selection. We report macro-averaged Accuracy, Precision, Recall, and F1, computed per outer test fold and then averaged. 1
