E. Ablation Study
To evaluate the contribution of each module, we conduct an ablation study on three variants: (i) Stage 1 only (no Stage 2 or ASA); (ii) Stage 2 only (no Stage 1 or ASA); (iii) the full model. The results (Fig. 2) show that both the dual-stage architecture and the Ambiguous Sample Augmenter (ASA) significantly enhance performance. The full model, incorporating both components, outperforms the variants with only Stage 1 or Stage 2, demonstrating the effectiveness of the combined approach in improving classification accuracy and robustness.
IV. CONCLUSION
This paper presents ASA-DSNet, a novel dual-stage learning framework for precise Parkinson’s disease diagnosis based on inertial data from smartwatches. By jointly addressing the challenge of misclassified and boundary samples through ambiguous sample augmentation and corrective refinement, ASA-DSNet demonstrates robust and generalizable performance across two multiclass datasets. By moving beyond binary PD/HC discrimination, these tasks enable finer-grained separation of disease entities and clearer quantification of symptom severity. Future work will explore the integration of multi-modal sensing data and real-world deployment in clinical environments.
